# 27. PYTHON SOCKET CONCEPT

Python provides networking through:

```python
import socket
```

A basic TCP connection concept is:

```python
import socket

s = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
```

This creates a TCP socket.

---

# 28. SAFE REVERSE-CONNECTION DEMONSTRATION

For a lab, you can demonstrate the networking concept without spawning a system shell.

### Listener

```python
import socket

HOST = "127.0.0.1"
PORT = 4444

server = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
server.bind((HOST, PORT))
server.listen(1)

print(f"Listening on {HOST}:{PORT}")

conn, addr = server.accept()
print("Connection from:", addr)

data = conn.recv(1024)
print("Received:", data.decode(errors="replace"))

conn.close()
server.close()
```

---

# 29. LAB CLIENT

The corresponding safe client only sends text.

```python
import socket

HOST = "127.0.0.1"
PORT = 4444

client = socket.create_connection((HOST, PORT), timeout=5)
client.sendall(b"Hello from the lab client\n")

client.close()
```

This demonstrates:

```text
Client
  ↓
TCP connection
  ↓
Listener
```

without creating a command shell.

---

# 30. WHY THIS DEMONSTRATION IS USEFUL

It teaches the fundamental reverse-shell networking model:

```text
Target/client
      ↓
Outbound TCP connection
      ↓
Listener
```

Once you understand this, tools such as Metasploit become much easier to understand.

---

# 31. ACTUAL SHELL CONCEPT

A real reverse shell connects the socket to a command interpreter.

Conceptually:

```text
Socket
  ↕
Shell
  ↕
Operating System
```

Data received through the network becomes shell input, and shell output goes back through the network.

Because this creates remote command execution, it should only be demonstrated inside an isolated lab.

